# The IP-hygiene denylist, by example.
#
# Copy this file to `.ip-denylist` and put the real names in it. That file is
# gitignored and must never be committed: a list of client names in a public
# repository is the disclosure this whole mechanism exists to prevent. This
# example is what stays in the tree, so a new checkout is told the list exists
# without being told what is on it.
#
#     cp .ip-denylist.example .ip-denylist
#     $EDITOR .ip-denylist
#
# One term per line. A `#` starts a comment. Matching is case-insensitive and
# on substrings, so `Contoso` also catches `contoso-logistics` and
# `CONTOSO_WMS_EXPORT.csv`.
#
# Put in anything whose appearance in a public commit would be a disclosure:
#
#   - every client and former client, in every spelling and abbreviation
#   - every employer, and its internal product and program code names
#   - internal system hostnames, share names, and ticket prefixes
#   - dataset and file names that came from somewhere other than this repository
#
# Short terms are the ones to be careful with. A two-letter program code will
# match inside ordinary words and make the gate something people bypass, which
# is worse than not having it. Prefer the longest unambiguous spelling.
#
# The generic half of the scan needs no list and runs everywhere, including on
# a CI runner where this file cannot exist. It looks for share-drive paths,
# document control numbers, and classification banners.

Contoso
Northwind Traders
PROJECT BLUEBIRD
wms-prod-01
